- op: discuss  # 4e74
  at: '2026-08-02T20:27:33Z'  # 4e74
  by: agent  # 4e74
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4e74
  clock: 3  # 4e74
  nonce: '4e74'  # 4e74
  message: "FIXED and merged, PR #168, dev e3310a3e3a.\n\n_module_matches is now COMPONENT-AWARE, with a different rule per direction and\nthe safety property named for each:\n * exact / component-PREFIX; where the prefix is strict, the first EXTRA\n   component decides - capitalised = a type inside the matched module\n   (os/exec + Cmd), lowercase = a sibling or sub-package (net/http + fcgi,\n   httptest, smtp) and must not match.\n * component-SUFFIX, unrestricted - dropped qualification. This is how source\n   spells these: Go writes http.Get after importing \"net/http\", Java writes\n   System.in for java.lang.System.in. Leading namespace cannot turn one module\n   into another the way an appended sub-package can.\n * Swift receiver-variable carve-out where the DIRECTION is the safety\n   property: the catalog may end with the hint, never the reverse.\n   'chaos'.endswith('os') is the bug; 'NonBlockingFileIO'.endswith('fileIO') is\n   the feature. Bounded to suffixes starting on a capital.\n\nTHE FILING'S PROPOSED FIX SHAPE WAS PARTLY WRONG, and this is the transferable\npart (L1: the remedy is a hypothesis too). It proposed plain component-prefix\nmatching. Two existing tests refuted pieces of that:\n - test_go_slash_vs_dot: the Go analyzer emits os.exec.Cmd for what the catalog\n   spells os/exec, so keeping / distinct from . - the obvious way to separate\n   net/http.Client (a type) from net/http/fcgi (a package) - does not work. The\n   separator is not evidence of a package boundary; Go's naming convention is.\n - test_java_in_matches_with_system_module: java.lang.System against a hint of\n   'System' is a component SUFFIX, not a prefix. Plain prefix matching would\n   have broken Go's most common call shape (http.Get) and Java's System.in.\nBoth were caught by tests that PREDATE this change, which is the argument for\nrunning the whole affected suite rather than the new class alone.\n\nEVIDENCE. The item's own filed repro re-runs clean - all five verified-live\ncollisions, all four fresh-substrate observations, and the zone-flipping pair\nsys.process.Process / sys.process.ProcessBuilder. 3436 tests pass, changed\nsource at 100% coverage, self-tree validation ratchet clean (so the shrink-only\nre-baseline this item anticipated was not needed).\n\nSELF-TREE IMPACT IS NIL, MEASURED: an A/B with the analysis cache cleared and a\npositive control on each arm (asserting the loaded predicate's verdict on\nos/chaos before measuring) gives 735 boundary calls both ways with a\nBYTE-IDENTICAL whole output. That null is expected rather than suspicious -\nevery observed defect was Go or JavaScript and this tree is Python, which is\nexactly why INV-karud refused to close on self-tree numbers.\n\nA FIRST ATTEMPT AT THAT A/B WAS DISCARDED as untrustworthy: it compared 735 to\n735 with its only validity check a grep for \"cm in em or em in cm\", a string\npresent in both the old CODE and the new DOCSTRING that quotes it, so it could\nnot distinguish the arms. Two identical numbers from an instrument that cannot\ntell the conditions apart is a stale read, not a null result.\n\nTAGGED awaits_bakeoff_validation. No self-tree number can validate this fix -\nits entire effect is on fresh substrate. The directed cohort must establish four\nthings, in order: (1) the positive control fires at all (count flows whose sink\nis an ambiguous short name; if zero, a clean result proves nothing); (2) the\nfive NAMED defects are gone, checked by name not by count; (3) NO TRUE FLOWS\nWERE LOST - the known tradeoff is that a lowercase extra component can\nunder-match in languages that do not signal types by case, and that has not been\nmeasured; (4) INV-karud's status. The 2026-08-01 before-outputs survive for all\n9 repos, so this is a true A/B on identical inputs rather than a re-run."  # 4e74
